16
00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:42.050
danceratopz: So, welcome everyone to ACDT number 103. Today is the 16th of November.

17
00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:49.069
danceratopz: 2026. I've just dropped, the agenda, or the issue for the call in the chat.

18
00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:58.630
danceratopz: And today we've got quite an important topic to discuss, which is basically the proposed EIP 8037 and EIP-8038 changes for Glamsterdam.

19
00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:06.699
danceratopz: As you all know, Glamsterdam brings a lot of important changes to Ethereum, including ePBS and Block Level Access Lists.

20
00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:17.069
danceratopz: But not only that, we snuck in another secret headliner called Scaling, which is another very large set of changes,

21
00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:23.599
danceratopz: But allows, or will allow Ethereum to scale from 60 million gas to 200 million per gas per block.

22
00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:38.170
danceratopz: And allow us to keep state growth under control. Unfortunately, rather late in our fork development process, we found out that the proposed changes in 8037 can break some smart contract invariance.

23
00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:45.100
danceratopz: And this affects contract behavior. So…

24
00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:54.959
danceratopz: We've invited, smart contract developers and app Devs today to come and talk to us about this, and give their inputs for these changes.

25
00:18:55.220 --> 00:19:09.039
danceratopz: It's important that we keep improving Ethereum and keeping it sustainable, but scaling isn't worth doing at any cost, obviously. I'm super excited to see so many people here,

26
00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:26.799
danceratopz: when I first got to Ethereum, I basically dabbled with smart contract development and was just super excited by just the ability to, like, deploy permissionlessly to the chain. That was really remarkable to me, and one of the reasons why I stuck around and became a protocol tester.

27
00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:35.529
danceratopz: At the EF, which is where I work now. So yeah, these changes were quite large, and

28
00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.349
danceratopz: Required quite a few trade-offs.

29
00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:50.200
danceratopz: So we've got a few people who are going to talk about this today. First off, Marius is going to give an overview of the current situation, and maybe the extent of these changes, and then we're going to hear from…

30
00:19:50.340 --> 00:20:00.080
danceratopz: Two smart contract audit teams, Chain Security and Dedaub, that have been investigating any contracts that have been affected by these changes.

31
00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:21.379
danceratopz: And then we're gonna hear, about the internal EF investigations, and follow that, we're gonna have, let me just check the agenda myself. We're gonna have an open Q&A, and then we're gonna see how Ethereum can better align the app layer and the protocol layer moving forward. So,

32
00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:28.209
danceratopz: Many thanks for coming today. I think Ivan recognized some names in the call, which is really nice.

33
00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:34.680
danceratopz: I'd like to hand over to Marius to give us an overview of the current situation and where we are at today.

34
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:35.780
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Yes.

35
00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:37.770
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Thank you very much, Dan.

36
00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:51.609
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Yeah, as Ben already said, multiple contracts were relying on invariants in the protocol that are modified by 8037 and 8038. So why did it come to this?

37
00:20:51.980 --> 00:21:06.739
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): We proposed 8037 and 8038 in 2025. It enables us to significantly scale Ethereum and Amsterdam by 3.5x, 3.3x, and even further in future hard forks.

38
00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:09.920
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): this 3.3x…

39
00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:19.629
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Improvement will significantly reduce the cost for most users, so even though your cost in gas might go up.

40
00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:25.190
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): For most use cases, the actual cost to the user will significantly go down.

41
00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:33.529
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): And not only will this reduce costs for most users, it will also enable new use cases for Ethereum.

42
00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:44.130
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): The EIPs break a few invariants that we didn't think people would still rely on, so, kind of the work went on.

43
00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:55.699
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): We wanted to do significant public outreach, but due to multiple reorogs at the EF and budget cuts, the timelines for audits and outreach slipped.

44
00:21:56.290 --> 00:22:04.749
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): I personally share a lot of blame for these failures, as the last remaining X scaling track lead that's still at the EF.

45
00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:11.060
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Fortunately, Chain Security made us aware of these contracts, and…

46
00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:16.370
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Following the report from, chain security, we started investigating our thoughts.

47
00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:25.400
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): We will hear from Dedaub and Chain Security on their findings, as well… as from Tony on the EF internal investigation.

48
00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:44.060
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): And the EF has mobilized significant resources to make sure that core Devs and the community will receive an accurate picture of how many things are broken, what are broken, how much value is at stake, are there potential mitigations, and what the cost of these mitigations would be.

49
00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:50.299
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Before we go there, I would like to remind, everyone of a few things.

50
00:22:50.550 --> 00:23:02.980
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Application developers are crucial for Ethereum to thrive. Ethereum is a platform primarily built for trustless and unstoppable applications.

51
00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:07.429
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): And providing a stable API for app developers is crucial.

52
00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:18.659
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): The API that we are providing right now is unfortunately underspecified, and there are a lot of things that we know that we have to change about the API very soon.

53
00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:31.080
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Vitalik wrote a… or published a blog post today, I think, that lists a few of those changes, like, just to call out one example, or, like, the most

54
00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:32.930
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Important example.

55
00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:39.189
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): All contracts that rely on EC Recover, are going to be broken by the PQ transition.

56
00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:45.059
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): So… Yeah.

57
00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:58.859
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): we need to trans… or we need to, transition Ethereum to PQ at some point, in order for everyone to be… to be secure. And,

58
00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:08.639
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): there will be a large fallout from this. So there are these changes to the protocol that are coming up, that will break, certain contracts.

59
00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:27.880
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): What we have to be much better at is communicating these potential changes, and engaging with the community as well as security researchers before these changes hit testnets, so we will never be in this situation, again. We will later also talk a bit about

60
00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:33.470
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Potential things that we can do in order to improve the situation there.

61
00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:41.469
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): But I would also, like, quickly like to comment on some of the proposed alternatives to 1837 and 1838.

62
00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:49.009
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): I've seen a few, and, like, AI makes it very easy to propose alternatives.

63
00:24:49.250 --> 00:25:09.069
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): That fix all the issues, but they do come with, significant drawbacks in… in UX for users, or for block producers, or increase the maintenance burden of the codebases, or… or are not, like, just not compatible with future changes.

64
00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:13.229
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): So, most of the proposed solution ended up something

65
00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:18.120
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): it's something that resembles kind of, like, a worse EOF spec.

66
00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:33.369
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): If we want to go into that direction of versioning the EVM, we should just do the full EF spec, but that is definitely not something that we can bolt onto Amsterdam or even Hegota in any realistic time.

67
00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:42.670
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Most of the proposed alternatives, also break other invariants in the protocol, which would require new rounds of investigation.

68
00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:53.009
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): And a lot of the proposals also primarily target invariants that aren't actually the ones that, where we saw most of the contracts breaking.

69
00:25:55.080 --> 00:26:12.320
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): To close this, this section out, the Ethereum community and the core devs have to make very hard decisions about the future of the protocol, basically all the time, but especially, around, around, like, these last years.

70
00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:25.280
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Our job is to weigh the needs of all participants in the network. It's a $220 billion ecosystem, so there's a lot of value at stake, as well as a very diverse set of stakeholders.

71
00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:31.509
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): That's why this is such a hard decision, 8037 and 8038.

72
00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:39.409
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): 8038 enabled significantly higher gas limits, thus significantly lower cost for most users.

73
00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:55.000
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): But we also have to take into consideration, that potentially modifying Lambert will create additional delays in the order of 4 to 6 months, for Hegota and the PQ transition and the whole roadmap afterwards.

74
00:26:55.600 --> 00:27:12.830
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): Ethereum is an unfinished project that will have to evolve significantly in the next years to face the challenges of PQ and AI, which means the amount of potentially broken invariants will increase before hopefully settling at some point in the future.

75
00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:23.699
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): And I would like to explicitly thank everyone who's coming on the call today, and also spent a lot of time in the last few weeks on this topic. Many people went

76
00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:43.320
Marius Van Der Wijden (M): well above and beyond to collect data in order to provide an accurate picture for an honest and fair debate today, and we will hear from, yeah, many different people, that have engaged in this, both in their work time and in their free time. So, thank you everyone for coming on here.

77
00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:55.000
danceratopz: Amazing. Thanks so much, Marius, for the… overview and undertakes that.

78
