84
00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:28.340
danceratopz: Yeah, perfect. Yeah, thanks a lot.

85
00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:45.279
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Amazing, thanks a lot. Yeah, so, hi everyone. We've been tasked by the EF to do mostly two things. On one side, find more protocol affected by 8037 and 803.8 changes.

86
00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:52.680
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And on the other side, to find more invariant or things breaking with Glamsterdam overall.

87
00:28:54.360 --> 00:29:10.999
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): So we combined two approaches. First, we had, Agent Swarm approach, which was searching protocol categories, starting from deployment list, verified source code, and public software.

88
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:16.399
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And we expanded then through on-chain activity and related implementation.

89
00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:31.069
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): A second search that we did was trace-based, so we took approximately one year of mainnet transaction, and this helped us find effective path across interacting contracts.

90
00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:40.020
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): We then investigated candidates by comparing Osaka and Amsterdam behavior through replays and constructed scenarios.

91
00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:46.140
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And, yeah, then we could check the impact, and who controls the mitigation, if any.

92
00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:52.350
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And, yeah, then some adversarial review of this.

93
00:29:52.600 --> 00:30:03.770
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): So, we do not think that the coverage is complete here for multiple reasons. For example, dormant path, unverified code, or private off-chain systems.

94
00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:08.470
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Also, I'll come to this a bit later, but comparing with

95
00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:20.829
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): The EF investigation results, we found that our search missed findings that the EF identified, so we would expect there are also further affected systems to be found.

96
00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:29.860
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And also one point is that we did not have assessed at all L2s here, so this is only Ethereum mainnet.

97
00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:49.319
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): So yeah, we found 152 findings, of which 76 were also found by the EF own internal search. I think this will be presented just a bit later in this call, which brought this overall search to a total of 200 findings.

98
00:30:49.490 --> 00:31:03.500
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And here, it's important to note that one finding is not one contract, but one class of affected contracts, so if it's deployed multiple times, then this will not count in these numbers.

99
00:31:04.930 --> 00:31:24.679
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): On the invariant breakdown, so I will not go much into details of each invariant, but what we found is that most findings are coming from Invariant 4 and 7, which are from gas cost increases. So, when hard-coded gas budget and reserve are no longer sufficient under Glamsterdam.

100
00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:34.929
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): So those failures are not triggered by the new reservoir or refill mechanism, but really by the increased gas costs. So…

101
00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:41.730
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): From what we found, the reservoir and the refill are kind of a minority of finding here.

102
00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:51.959
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): On the mitigation analysis, so we found that the majority of the finding can be mitigated rather easily.

103
00:31:52.610 --> 00:32:05.900
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): However, there is still a few that requires either a contract upgrade, or in the case of a really immutable protocol, that will be a full redeployment of the system, because

104
00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:08.100
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): One cannot upgrade those.

105
00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:23.360
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): We also found some compatibility risks beyond this main finding set. On one side for, Gloas, so the consensus part of Glamsterdam.

106
00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:32.190
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): We identified, it'll have an issue across staking protocol and supporting service, where changes to…

107
00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:45.169
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Proof layout can block staking operation, and also moving execution data can break some indexer and Oracle, for example. So for this, we will also coordinate with the EF to report those.

108
00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:50.199
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And, the second one is,

109
00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:58.659
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): fixed gas limit for east transfer, so we found that some systems were assuming that,

110
00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:04.210
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): sending ETH to a new account, or generally to an account without coding code.

111
00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:13.790
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): They will assume that it should always succeed with 21,000 DAS, and this is no longer the case for a new account, because…

112
00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:18.079
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Now some account creation cost is being charged.

113
00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:32.579
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And yeah, so summary and next step, so, yeah, with some AI-based search, we managed to find 150 findings, both on-chain and off-chain.

114
00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:36.390
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Grass is also, breaking stuff.

115
00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:46.159
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): We took some time to write a draft guide on how to fix all of this try-catch pattern, which,

116
00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:49.160
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Which are the issue here.

117
00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:58.459
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And we're happy to get feedback on this, because we feel, gas repricing will happen in the future, and people should,

118
00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:07.829
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Make those try-catch robust in the future, otherwise we are gonna have this whole conversation once again in a few months or few years.

119
00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:17.209
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And, yeah, we would also recommend thinking about the other EIPs, especially for app Devs, to look into the whole,

120
00:34:17.719 --> 00:34:22.520
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): Glance to them, and check if something else might break.

121
00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.639
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): In the application layer.

122
00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:28.980
Tanguy (ChainSecurity): And, yeah, thank you, that's… from outside.

